    "payments_on_prepetition_debt": "no — April disbursements were post-petition payroll (4/10 and 4/24 cycles, both for work performed entirely after the 3/10/2026 petition; the 3/13 payroll covering the straddling 2/28–3/13 period was paid in March under Dkt. 9) and ordinary-course post-petition vendor payments (11 U.S.C. §§ 503(b)(1), 363(c)(1)). The Mesa Steel February invoice was NOT paid; Mesa shipped the Q2 order under the court-recognized post-petition trade agreement (Dkt. 83) and the February invoice remains an unsecured pre-petition claim. Holland & Pierce interim-compensation ripened 4/29 under Dkt. 77 (80% of $140,000 fees + 100% of $4,200 expenses = $116,200.00) but no payment is recorded through 4/30; a Dkt. 77 interim payment would be an administrative expense in any event, not pre-petition debt.",
    "payments_outside_ordinary_course_without_approval": "no — payroll and vendor payments are ordinary course (§ 363(c)(1)); the 4/9 DIP draw of $500,000 is an approved borrowing under Dkt. 61 (cumulative draws $1,550,000 of the $2,500,000 facility); the Mesa post-petition trade agreement is a court-recognized order (Dkt. 83); no professional-fee payment was made without §§ 330/331 procedures."
  }
}

